status: open

---

Support multiple ticket folders in Visual Studio Code.

The VS Code extension currently supports one ticket directory in the first workspace folder. It
should allow one project to configure multiple ticket directories. For example, a project could
use both `tickets` and `tickets/private`.

The configuration should contain an ordered list of ticket folders. Each item should have a label
and a path relative to the first workspace folder. For example:

    "ticketfile.ticketFolders": [
        {
            "label": "Tickets",
            "path": "tickets"
        },
        {
            "label": "Private",
            "path": "tickets/private"
        }
    ]

When more than one ticket folder is configured, the tree should have one top-level item for each
configured folder. Status groups and tickets should appear below it. Folder order should follow
configuration order. When only one folder is configured, the extension should keep the current
tree layout without an extra ticket-folder level.

Ticket IDs can overlap between configured folders. Folder ownership should remain attached to each
group and ticket item so actions always use the correct path.

Creating a ticket should use the selected ticket-folder item when one is available. If no target
can be inferred and more than one ticket folder is configured, the extension should ask the user
to select a ticket folder.

Each configured ticket folder should have its own file watcher. File changes, filtering, counters,
status changes, comments, deletion, and automatic refresh should work for all configured folders.

The existing `ticketfile.ticketsFolder` setting should be removed. The default `ticketFolders`
configuration should contain one folder with the label `Tickets` and the path `tickets`.

Ticket folder paths must not be empty or duplicated. Multi-root workspace support is not part of
this work item. All configured paths are relative to the first workspace folder.

